---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Answer from '../components/Answer.astro';
import Faq from '../components/Faq.astro';

const faqs = [
  { q: 'Will they “just seal if I tighten more”?', a: 'No. Extra torque on a 14-into-15 joint crushes the O-ring without filling the extra millimetre. You get a spray cut at the nut.' },
  { q: 'Which size should I standardize on?', a: 'If you are building a kit from scratch, M22-14 mm plus 3/8″ QC is the easier aftermarket path. If the pump is 15 mm, put a 15→14 adapter on the pump and then standardize.' },
  { q: 'Is 15 mm only electric?', a: 'Mostly, not exclusively. AR Blue Clean documents both on their own equipment. Measure.' },
];
---
<Base title="M22 14mm vs 15mm" description="M22-14 and M22-15 share a 22 mm thread. The 1 mm stem difference is why a new hose threads on and then leaks." path="/m22-14-vs-15/">
  <div class="prose">
    <p class="crumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/m22/">M22</a> / 14 vs 15</p>
    <h1>M22-14 mm vs M22-15 mm</h1>
    <Answer>
      Same thread, different stem. 14 mm into 15 mm leaks. 15 mm into 14 mm will not go. Use a dedicated 14↔15 coupler — not more wrench.
    </Answer>

    <div class="diagram" aria-hidden="true">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 640 160" width="100%" height="160">
        <text x="8" y="22" font-size="14" fill="#5c564c">Female bore</text>
        <rect x="20" y="40" width="120" height="70" fill="none" stroke="#1c1914" stroke-width="2"/>
        <rect x="50" y="55" width="60" height="40" fill="#fde68a" stroke="#b45309"/>
        <text x="58" y="80" font-size="12">14 mm</text>
        <rect x="180" y="40" width="120" height="70" fill="none" stroke="#1c1914" stroke-width="2"/>
        <rect x="205" y="52" width="70" height="46" fill="#fde68a" stroke="#b45309"/>
        <text x="218" y="80" font-size="12">15 mm</text>
        <text x="340" y="22" font-size="14" fill="#5c564c">Male stem (same M22 thread)</text>
        <rect x="360" y="48" width="90" height="54" fill="#e7e5e4" stroke="#1c1914"/>
        <rect x="450" y="58" width="70" height="34" fill="#fed7aa" stroke="#9a3412"/>
        <text x="458" y="80" font-size="12">14 or 15</text>
      </svg>
      <p style="margin:0;color:#5c564c;font-size:0.9rem;">The yellow hole is the seal. The outer rectangle is the thread you see.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>What happens at pressure</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Stem</th><th>Bore</th><th>Hand-tight feel</th><th>At 1500+ PSI</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>14</td><td>14</td><td>Snug, O-ring disappears</td><td>Dry if the ring is intact</td></tr>
        <tr><td>15</td><td>15</td><td>Snug</td><td>Dry</td></tr>
        <tr><td>14</td><td>15</td><td>Threads easily, slight wiggle</td><td>Fan spray at the nut</td></tr>
        <tr><td>15</td><td>14</td><td>Stops early</td><td>Do not start the pump</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Why the internet contradicts itself</h2>
    <p>Some pages say 14 mm is electric and 15 mm is gas. Manufacturer pages go the other way: AR Blue Clean and a lot of aftermarket hose makers treat <strong>14 mm as the gas / aftermarket default</strong> and <strong>15 mm as a common electric outlet</strong> (Sun Joe support describes M22-15 mm on the unit outlet). Believe the caliper.</p>

    <h2>The cheap fix</h2>
    <p>Brass M22-15 mm male × M22-14 mm female (or the reverse) couplers are sold as “Sun Joe adapters” even when your machine is not a Sun Joe. Check:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Which side is 15 mm (that side faces the 15 mm machine or hose).</li>
      <li>PSI rating ≥ your machine.</li>
      <li>A replaceable O-ring, not a molded-in smear of rubber.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>After the sizes match, you can add <a href="/m22-to-quick-connect/">M22 → quick-connect</a> and stop threading guns in the driveway.</p>
    <Faq items={faqs} />
  </div>
</Base>
